{"name":"Channel","hex":"#f1c3c2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Channel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Ellipsis of English Channel: a strait in Europe, separating Great Britain from France and connecting the North Sea to the Atlantic Ocean."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A former village and district of Channel-Port aux Basques, Newfoundland, Newfoundland and Labrador, Canada."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Channel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Channel (geography)","blocks":[{"text":"In physical geography and hydrology, a channel is a landform on which a relatively narrow body of water is situated, such as a river, river delta or strait. While channel typically refers to a natural formation, the cognate term canal denotes a similar artificial structure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Vivari_Channel.jpg/330px-Vivari_Channel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_(geography)","wikidata":"Q1210950"},{"title":"Channel Country","blocks":[{"text":"The Channel Country is a region of outback Australia mostly in the state of Queensland but also in parts of South Australia, Northern Territory and New South Wales. The name comes from the numerous intertwined rivulets that cross the region, which cover 150,000 km². The Channel Country is over the Cooper and Eromanga geological basins and the Lake Eyre Basin drainage basin. Further to the east is the less arid Maranoa district."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/IBRA_6.1_Channel_Country.png/330px-IBRA_6.1_Channel_Country.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Channel_Country","wikidata":"Q1062252"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Lake_George_Channel.jpg/500px-Lake_George_Channel.jpg?utm_source